1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,517
Hello! Last time we learned the vowels.

2
00:00:02,517 --> 00:00:06,130
Today we meet the other hero of Hangeul: the consonants.

3
00:00:06,130 --> 00:00:08,260
What shapes were they modeled on?

4
00:00:14,130 --> 00:00:14,684
Amazingly,

5
00:00:14,684 --> 00:00:19,006
Hangeul consonants were modeled on the very organs that make sound: the mouth,

6
00:00:19,006 --> 00:00:20,502
the tongue, and the throat.

7
00:00:20,502 --> 00:00:22,220
This is the principle of shape.

8
00:00:29,030 --> 00:00:29,778
First, "ㄱ" [g].

9
00:00:29,778 --> 00:00:33,312
It shows the root of the tongue blocking the throat.

10
00:00:33,312 --> 00:00:36,710
Say the sound: '그' [geu]. It sounds near the back teeth.

11
00:00:40,060 --> 00:00:43,494
"ㄴ" [n] shows the tongue tip touching the upper gum.

12
00:00:43,494 --> 00:00:43,780
'느' [neu].

13
00:00:43,780 --> 00:00:47,500
The tongue gently meets the front roof of the mouth.

14
00:00:48,670 --> 00:00:52,057
"ㅁ" [m] is the shape of closed lips. '므' [meu].

15
00:00:52,057 --> 00:00:54,620
The two lips meet and close.

16
00:00:56,820 --> 00:00:59,780
"ㅅ" [s] is the shape of a tooth. '스' [seu].

17
00:00:59,780 --> 00:01:02,650
Air slips out between the teeth.

18
00:01:05,090 --> 00:01:07,554
"ㅇ" [ng] is the round shape of the throat.

19
00:01:07,554 --> 00:01:10,350
As a final sound it becomes the nasal '응' [eung].

20
00:01:10,350 --> 00:01:13,080
At the start, it is a silent placeholder.

21
00:01:17,080 --> 00:01:21,611
So the five basic consonants — "ㄱ" [g], "ㄴ" [n], "ㅁ" [m], "ㅅ" [s],

22
00:01:21,611 --> 00:01:25,960
"ㅇ" [ng] — were born from the shapes of our own body.

23
00:01:27,610 --> 00:01:28,807
What about the rest?

24
00:01:28,807 --> 00:01:31,860
A very clever method: when the sound gets stronger,

25
00:01:31,860 --> 00:01:34,195
you add one stroke to the basic letter.

26
00:01:34,195 --> 00:01:36,350
This is the stroke-adding principle.

27
00:01:43,830 --> 00:01:50,737
Add a stroke to "ㄴ" [n] and you get "ㄷ" [d] ; add to "ㅁ" [m] and you get "ㅂ" [b].

28
00:01:50,737 --> 00:01:54,730
The sound becomes clearer and firmer.

29
00:01:55,180 --> 00:02:02,130
Add a stroke to "ㅅ" [s] for "ㅈ" [j], and to "ㅇ" [ng] for "ㅎ" [h].

30
00:02:02,130 --> 00:02:09,080
And the rolling "ㄹ" [r] too. Do you see the pattern?

31
00:02:11,470 --> 00:02:17,209
Here are the ten basic consonants for today: "ㄱ" [g],

32
00:02:17,209 --> 00:02:22,362
"ㄴ" [n], "ㄷ" [d], "ㄹ" [r], "ㅁ" [m], "ㅂ" [b], "ㅅ" [s], "ㅇ" [ng], "ㅈ" [j], "ㅎ" [h].

33
00:02:22,362 --> 00:02:26,110
Let's call their names together.

34
00:02:27,380 --> 00:02:28,992
Now the most magical part!

35
00:02:28,992 --> 00:02:34,384
Hangeul does not write consonant and vowel separately — it gathers them into one block.

36
00:02:34,384 --> 00:02:36,120
This is 'syllable assembly'.

37
00:02:42,040 --> 00:02:45,013
The consonant is the first sound,

38
00:02:45,013 --> 00:02:47,445
the vowel the middle sound.

39
00:02:47,445 --> 00:02:52,310
Put first "ㄱ" [g] and middle "ㅏ" [a] together and you get '가' [ga].

40
00:02:58,110 --> 00:03:05,239
The rule is simple. Upright vowels like ㅏ, ㅓ,

41
00:03:05,239 --> 00:03:13,160
ㅣ go to the right of the consonant — like 나, 머, 디.

42
00:03:16,200 --> 00:03:22,617
In contrast, flat vowels like ㅗ, ㅜ,

43
00:03:22,617 --> 00:03:29,950
ㅡ go below the consonant — like 고, 누, 드.

44
00:03:31,870 --> 00:03:35,211
Today we use only letters without a final consonant.

45
00:03:35,211 --> 00:03:36,111
With no final,

46
00:03:36,111 --> 00:03:38,810
the sound stays open — an 'open syllable'.

47
00:03:43,020 --> 00:03:45,146
Let's build a word.

48
00:03:45,146 --> 00:03:50,069
Write '고' [go] and '기' [gi] side by side: '고기' [go-gi] — meat.

49
00:03:50,069 --> 00:03:53,650
Two blocks join to make meaning!

50
00:03:56,500 --> 00:04:05,061
Let's name family. 'ㄴ' [n] and 'ㅏ' [a] make '나' [na], and '누나' [nu-na].

51
00:04:05,061 --> 00:04:11,910
'오빠' [o-ppa] and '누나' [nu-na] are warm words for family.

52
00:04:16,770 --> 00:04:20,359
머리 means head. 바나나 means banana.

53
00:04:20,359 --> 00:04:25,630
The consonants and vowels snap together neatly!

54
00:04:43,570 --> 00:04:46,718
아버지 (father) and 어머니 (mother).

55
00:04:46,718 --> 00:04:49,867
Even with only open syllables,

56
00:04:49,867 --> 00:04:52,910
we can write such warm words.

57
00:04:53,740 --> 00:04:57,388
오이 (cucumber), 호수 (lake),

58
00:04:57,388 --> 00:05:02,642
지도 (map) — easy open-syllable words.

59
00:05:02,642 --> 00:05:06,290
Read them aloud after me.

60
00:05:06,740 --> 00:05:10,912
Now, flashcard time! '고기' [go-gi], '호수' [ho-su], '기차' [gi-cha].

61
00:05:10,912 --> 00:05:12,998
Read them out loud,

62
00:05:12,998 --> 00:05:16,840
recalling each consonant and vowel.

63
00:05:19,890 --> 00:05:23,234
Try writing each consonant stroke by stroke with your pencil.

64
00:05:23,234 --> 00:05:26,250
Writing by hand helps you remember the shape and sound.

65
00:05:29,000 --> 00:05:32,566
Today we learned ten basic consonants and how to assemble blocks.

66
00:05:32,566 --> 00:05:34,760
Now you can read and write simple words!

67
00:05:39,990 --> 00:05:43,482
Practice a little every day and your Hangeul will grow fast.

68
00:05:43,482 --> 00:05:46,042
Subscribe and like to join the next journey.

69
00:05:46,042 --> 00:05:47,090
See you next time!

